{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And to them you shall say, etc.</b> In tractate Zevaḥim (Zevaḥim 107a) it is taught in a baraita, Rabbi Yishmael says: to join the parashiyyot, to say that even slaughtered offerings outside [the Temple precinct] that he brought up outside, even though they are not fit for inside — such as one who slaughters inside and brings up outside, as is taught in a baraita in the Mishnah at the beginning of chapter 13 of Zevaḥim — and to apply everything stated regarding this one to that one, apart from matters that the verse expanded in one of them and restricted in the other. And see the words of Rashi in Zevaḥim." "<b>Any man, etc.</b> See the verse before this one." "<b>From the house of Israel.</b> It was necessary to say ״מִבֵּית יִשְׂרָאֵל״ and the expression ״וַאֲלֵיהֶ״ was not sufficient, from which one would understand that it refers back to those mentioned above concerning the matter. This is because it said ״אִישׁ אִישׁ״ to mean two people who slaughtered, as I wrote nearby, and the verse was concerned lest one who expounds come and expound it to include the nations, as they expound (MeNaḤot 73a) on ״אִישׁ אִישׁ״ stated regarding vows and freewill offerings (Leviticus 22:18) — that it comes to include the nations. Therefore it was necessary to say ״מִבֵּית״ etc. — specifically Israel and not the nations, and consequently it became necessary to expound from ״אִישׁ אִישׁ״ everything that our Sages of blessed memory expounded. And once it was necessary to say ״מִבֵּית יִשְׂרָאֵל,״ the verse was concerned to indicate that the verse excluded the converts, therefore it says ״וּמִן הַגֵּר״." "And it was necessary to include all the inclusions, for otherwise one might say that it included only what it mentioned in the verse. And also according to the opinion of Rabbi Yosi, who maintains that the Torah spoke in the language of men, perhaps he said this only because there is an impediment to the exposition on account of that restriction. And although the Gemara raised a difficulty against Rabbi Yosi — that since he does not expound ״אִישׁ אִישׁ״ in the second parashah, likewise ״אִישׁ אִישׁ״ in the first parashah he would not expound — even so, if the Gemara had not found the exposition of ״דָּם שָׁפַךְ,״ it would have said that it is expounded from ״אִישׁ אִישׁ,״ for whenever there is no opposition to the exposition we do not say ״the Torah spoke [in the language of men]״ etc. And see the words of the Tosafot in Meẓi'a, folio 31a, s.v. דִּבְרָה תּוֹרָה כִּלְשׁוֹן בְּנֵי אָדָם, that it is not reasonable to say that the Tanna does not hold of the several expositions that are derived from the doubling, and although the Tosafot specified the difficulty for Rabbi Shim'on, nevertheless it applies to all. And the principal dispute among the Tannaim is in reasoning concerning a matter that opposes the exposition: according to the reasoning one is inclined to say ״the Torah spoke in the language of men,״ and you will find that even Rabbi Shim'on — we find that he said (Bava Meẓi'a 31a) ״the Torah spoke in the language of men״ and we find that he expounds the doubling."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:57:52.777873980Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}